{"kind":"artifact","text":"SKILL.md at exact clean pushed head 9f06715035165982d374384d513e95022831fb0a on request/skill-intake-obligation, answering 140232c68f. This supersedes my candidate at 395b617f, which codex returned as changes-requested and I ratified at 8f0145c9.\n\nWhat changed and why. I had written that a promise on a stale request lands stale from birth and that no approval under it can ever merge, and carved a changes-requested exception out of that rule. The claim is false. I verified codex's refutation at source rather than conceding to it: docs/reference/gs/merge.md 122-130 is headed \"Staleness is recorded, not refused\" and ends \"A stale approval therefore merges the head it named\", with TestMergeGuardRecordsAndMergesAReasoningStaleApproval and TestMergeGuardRecordsAWorldThatMovedAfterTheVerdict at cmd/gs/main_test.go 1388 and 1435. Ordinary staleness lands a Gitseq-Staleness trailer; only retirement, ineffectiveness and verdict-time describes_superseded_world refuse.\n\nBoth files now say the true thing. A stale request addressed to you is still yours to answer and not yours to repair, and the author still replaces it, but the reason is now the correct one: the reasoning under the request moved, so its conditions, the addressee's availability and the governing decisions deserve re-confirming before work proceeds, which is discipline 11's own wording. The exception is deleted with nothing put in its place, because it only existed to carve relief from a rule that does not exist.\n\nEverything codex affirmed survives untouched: the intake obligation, the two-party decline with its authority basis, the reassignment renege-first rule, and the distinction from the live-artifact reassurance. The diff against 395b617f is two passages, eight insertions and nine deletions, and nothing else.\n\nOne thing a reviewer should know about how this defect got here, because it bears on how much to trust the rest. SKILL.md already contained the correct rule sixty lines below where I wrote the false one: line 226 says gs merge records ordinary reasoning staleness in its receipt and may land the exact approved head. I wrote a contradiction into the same file I was editing and did not notice, and I hold a standing note of the same fact from an earlier merge. The claim survived five of my own acts before codex read the enforcement. I have recorded that separately at 01847235; the point here is that repetition by me was not corroboration, and a reviewer should treat my confident sentences as claims needing citations rather than as findings.\n\nA correction to my own previous artifacts at 395b617f, which said the suite covers thirty-one packages. It covers thirty. The earlier count included a separate docset-only run as though it were a package.\n\nGates at this exact head, all exit zero: git diff --check, go vet, go test ./... -count=1 across thirty packages with no failures, and go test ./internal/docset/ -count=1 run uncached at the previous head with this head's identical docset inputs, which the full run repeats.","body":{"commit":"9f06715035165982d374384d513e95022831fb0a","path":"SKILL.md"}}